  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 AM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nCurrently, southwest flow persists over the Northern Plains \nfrom a large trough over the western CONUS. At the surface a \nshallow surface low sits in northeast Wyoming. This southwest \nflow has created a large area of high clouds over most of the \nnorth central CONUS. There is low confidence in patchy fog this\nmorning as well, likely just in the valleys. With the surface \nlow and upper level southwest flow, today will be another day \nabove normal with highs in the 50s.\n\nHeading into Wednesday the southwest flow will stretch and spin\na Colorado Low off the Rocky Mountain high peaks, ejecting it\ninto the eastern Northern Plains. The shortwave and surface low\nis forecast to pass right through North Dakota. This in turn\nwill keep the QPF across the western and northern portions of\nthe state. With temperatures mainly above freezing, this will be\na light rain event. QPF amounts range from 0.10\" in the \nnorthwest and far north, to almost nothing elsewhere. This \nsystem also represents a downward turn for temperatures in \nNorth Dakota, with highs dropping back down into the 30s. Gusty\nwest to northwest winds, sustained around 25 to 35 MPH and \ngusting possibly as high as 50 MPH are expected from the tight \npressure gradient. Moving forward, wind headlines will likely \nbe needed in at least the southwest, either warning or advisory.\nHowever, looking at the EFI values for a windy Wednesday, they \nbarely support a Windy Advisory in the far southwest with values\nonly around 0.70 (0.80 is a typical advisory day). Being that \nthe center of the low moves through the state during the day, \nthe extreme winds are going to be in Montana and South Dakota\nduring peak mixing. The high pressure bubble is also weak behind\nthe low. By the time the stronger pressure gradient moves into \nthe state, it will be after sunset and the boundary layer still \nstart to separate from the surface. The raw NBM is very \naggressive on the winds, so we limited them to just below \nwarning criteria, and will continue to monitor. \n\nConfidence continue for rain and snow again Friday through \nSaturday as a strong Pacific Northwestern low crosses over the \nRockies and slides across the Northern Plains, enhanced by the \nRockies. This time the low track look more likely to move \nthrough South Dakota, leading to widespread precipitation across\nthe Dakotas. With the cyclonic flow of the system, central and \neastern North Dakota will mostly stay above freezing, while \nwestern and northern North Dakota will see colder air being \nwrapped into the low right away. Therefor through most of the \nday Friday the rain/snow line will be a diagonal from Bowman to \nDevils lake. Late Friday night all temperatures will drop below \nfreezing leading to widespread snow falling heading into \nSaturday morning as the system pulls away to the east.\n\nSnow amounts will be tricky to forecast as the rain/snow line\nmay move. Currently the NBM probabilities for 1 inch are 30 to\n50 percent from the diagonal previous mentioned, with the \nhigher probabilities in the north central. For 2 inches it \nfocuses on the north central with a 40 percent chance. The 4 and\n6 inch prob are both around 25 percent in north central from \nabout Highway 2, up to Canada. Now of course ground \ntemperatures and the speed of the system will dictate how much \nactually accumulates. This storm has been in the models for a \nlong time, but now the NBM probabilities for accumulating snow \nare really increasing! High temperatures through the weekend \nthen will stay in the 20s to 30s. Expect highs to be more so in\nthe 20s where snow does accumulate.",
